<title>USA: U.S. Q3 non-farm productivity up 0.2 percent.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q3 non-farm productivity up 0.2 percent.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Dept preliminary estimates of Q3 1996 U.S. productivity/costs, seasonally adjusted annual percent changes from prior quarters, on chain weights basis:</p>
<p>    -	     - Productivity -	  - - -Output- - -</p>
<p>    -	     Q3-P   Q2-R  Q1-R	 Q3-P   Q2-R  Q1</p>
<p> NonFarm	   0.2   0.5   1.7	   2.3   4.8   2.7</p>
<p> Manufacturing   6.3   2.9   5.6	   5.4   7.1   2.4</p>
<p> Durable Goods   8.2   3.7   7.8	   7.5  10.7   5.5</p>
<p> NonDurables     3.7   2.0   3.0	   2.7   2.4  -1.5</p>
<p> Total Business  0.3   1.1   2.0	   2.4   5.0   3.0</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>    -	     - - -Hours- - -	Hourly Compensation</p>
<p>    -	     Q3-P    Q2    Q1	  Q3-P    Q2    Q1</p>
<p> NonFarm	   2.1   4.3   1.0	   3.9   3.8   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing  -0.8   4.1  -3.1	   4.6   5.8   0.4</p>
<p> Durable Goods  -0.7   6.7  -2.1	   4.6   6.8  -2.2</p>
<p> NonDurables    -1.0   0.4  -4.4	   4.7   3.6   4.6</p>
<p> Total Business  2.0   3.9   1.0	   4.3   4.1   3.0</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.  </p>
<p>    -	 Real Hourly Compensation    Unit Labor Costs</p>
<p>    -	     Q3-P    Q2    Q1	  Q3-P    Q2    Q1</p>
<p> NonFarm	   1.6  UNCH  UNCH	   3.7   3.3   1.5</p>
<p> Manufacturing   2.2   1.9  -2.8	  -1.6   2.8  -4.9</p>
<p> Durable Goods   2.2   2.9  -5.3	  -3.3   3.0  -9.3</p>
<p> NonDurables     2.3  -0.2   1.3	   0.9   1.6   1.6</p>
<p> Total Business  2.0   0.3  -0.2	   4.0   3.0   1.0</p>
<p>P-Preliminary. R-Revised.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast third quarter non-farm productivity to fall 0.1 percent and unit labor costs to be up 3.7 percent.</p>
<p>Manufacturing productivity jumped 6.3 percent in the third quarter, the largest monthly increase since the first quarter of December 1994, when it rose 6.7 percent, the department said.</p>
<p>Compared to the third quarter of 1995, non-farm productivity rose 0.3 percent in the third quarter, output rose 2.5 percent, hours increased 2.2 percent, hourly compensation was up 3.8 percent, real compensation increased 0.8 percent and unit labor costs advanced 3.4 percent, the department said.</p>
